# Intent: The unit tells the host which way is down, so the host can show the tilted, upside-down and face-down looks again (owner 2026-11-16: tilt, roll to the side and pick-up reactions worked on the build before the rebuild and are gone). Ports the orientation tracker of legacy patches 067, 071 and 072 as one change: the accelerometer is low-passed (0.5), the dominant axis counts only with a pull of at least 6.5 m/s^2 and a total between 0.8 and 1.2 g (carrying or shaking is ignored), and a new axis replaces the old pose only when it beats the signed pull in the old pose's direction by 2 and holds for five samples (a pose shows in about half a second, and turning from one ear to the other no longer locks the tracker). Each change, and once a minute while it stays, pushes an "orientation" event {axis, gx, gy, gz in milli-g}; the host maps the axis to a pose (host/maipai_body/bodies/stackchan/orientation.py). The pure logic is maipai_orient_core.h, tested by host/tests/firmware/orient_core_test.c. The screen-rotation tool of legacy 067 is left out on purpose: the host keeps SCREEN_ROTATION_ENABLED off because the panel mirror command froze the face.
# Placement: unit (the sensor is on the unit; the host only maps the axis to a face)
# Upstream: m5stack/StackChan firmware/main/hal/hal_imu.cpp (MIT); new hal/maipai_orient_core.h (MIT)
# Check after a flash: leave the unit upright, read self.robot.get_events: an "orientation" event with the axis of its resting pose arrives within a second of boot; tip it onto one ear, then the other, then upright: each is an event within about a second and the host log shows the pose face; carrying it across the room makes no flurry.
